% Goal

Implement and maintain `brain/transition_overrides.py` as the Sparse override layer at plans/<slug>/transitions.json, stored as a list KEYED BY THE (from_track_id, to_track_id) PAIR rather than by index, so an override survives unrelated reordering. merge(segments, overrides) patches only non-null fields onto the derived segments and sets overridden plus override_fields, keeping the beatgrid/phrase derivation authoritative. NULL MEANS LEAVE UNCHANGED AND {clear:true} MEANS DELETE. This is a DELIBERATE DIVERGENCE FROM RFC 7396 JSON Merge Patch, where null deletes; state it explicitly in generated code and docstrings, because the LLM path is exactly where merge-patch semantics would be wrongly assumed and {'beats': null} would silently destroy the beat count. reconcile(order, overrides) marks a pair that is no longer adjacent as orphaned and RESTORES it to active on re-adjacency, so removing and re-adding a track restores its transitions. technique and showcase_move are free strings, never closed enums, because build_mix_plan.py:1288 composes f'dj_format_{recipe}' at runtime and an enum breaks the builder. upsert persists author on the record itself so an LLM-written transition is distinguishable from a hand-written one at rest, not only in the journal. upsert and clear each append exactly one journal entry; reconcile called as part of another module's write journals NOTHING of its own and is recorded in that write's detail, or the What-changed strip fills with noise on every reorder.

% Product contract

- Preserve the exact public interface declared in `<pdd-interface>`.
- Treat the reviewed architecture description in this prompt as normative behavior.
- Keep plan identity, immutable track identity, revision guards, and non-destructive storage semantics intact across GUI, CLI, and agent callers.
- Preserve compatible behavior outside this module's stated responsibility; do not redesign unrelated mixing or playback logic.
- Reject malformed, stale, ambiguous, or unsafe writes explicitly instead of silently losing plan data.
- Keep the implementation deterministic where the contract describes deterministic behavior.
- Target Python 3.13+ for Python modules and baseline browser JavaScript without a bundler for browser modules.
- Inspect the current implementation, its declared dependency modules, and the focused tests before changing code. This is a brownfield source prompt: working, tested behavior not contradicted here remains part of the contract.

% Architecture detail

Sparse override layer at plans/<slug>/transitions.json, stored as a list KEYED BY THE (from_track_id, to_track_id) PAIR rather than by index, so an override survives unrelated reordering. merge(segments, overrides) patches only non-null fields onto the derived segments and sets overridden plus override_fields, keeping the beatgrid/phrase derivation authoritative. NULL MEANS LEAVE UNCHANGED AND {clear:true} MEANS DELETE. This is a DELIBERATE DIVERGENCE FROM RFC 7396 JSON Merge Patch, where null deletes; state it explicitly in generated code and docstrings, because the LLM path is exactly where merge-patch semantics would be wrongly assumed and {'beats': null} would silently destroy the beat count. reconcile(order, overrides) marks a pair that is no longer adjacent as orphaned and RESTORES it to active on re-adjacency, so removing and re-adding a track restores its transitions. technique and showcase_move are free strings, never closed enums, because build_mix_plan.py:1288 composes f'dj_format_{recipe}' at runtime and an enum breaks the builder. upsert persists author on the record itself so an LLM-written transition is distinguishable from a hand-written one at rest, not only in the journal. upsert and clear each append exactly one journal entry; reconcile called as part of another module's write journals NOTHING of its own and is recorded in that write's detail, or the What-changed strip fills with noise on every reorder.

